/**
	Generates tones, notes and rests into a buffer in memory, from which a sound, an array of samples or a WAV file is made, as BGT's tone_synth did.
	tone_synth();
	## Remarks:
		The audio is 16-bit stereo at 44100 hertz, as `sample_rate` and `channels` read. The notes and tones are written starting at the current position, `position` or `position_ms`, and leave the position where it was, as in BGT, so that notes written one after another sound together as a chord; `rest()` and `rest_ms()` move the position on, which is how a melody is written: a note, then a rest as long as it, then the next. Writing over audio already there mixes the two. `seek()`, `seek_ms()`, `rewind()` and `rewind_ms()` move the position anywhere from the start to the end of what is written so far, the end itself included, and return false for a position outside that; a rest at the end makes the audio longer.
		Lengths are given in beats or, for the methods ending in `_ms`, in milliseconds. A beat lasts 60000 / `tempo` milliseconds, 500 at the default tempo of 120.
		### Settings
		* int waveform_type: the shape of the wave, 1 for sawtooth, 2 for square, 3 for sine and 4 for triangle, as BGT numbered them. Other values are ignored. The default is 3, a sine wave.
		* double volume: in decibels, from -100 to 0, the default. Other values are ignored.
		* double pan: from -100, fully left, to 100, fully right; 0 by default. Other values are ignored.
		* double tempo: in beats per minute, from 1 to 999; 120 by default. Other values are ignored.
		* double note_transpose: semitones added to every note, fractions of one included; 0 by default.
		* double freq_transpose: hertz added to every frequency given to `freq()` and its variants; 0 by default.
		* bool allow_silent_output: whether a buffer holding only silence, as after only `rest()`, can be written out; false by default, which makes the write methods fail for it.
		* bool set_edge_fades(int start, int end): the fade in and out of each tone, in milliseconds, 8 and 12 by default, which keep tones from clicking. Negative values are refused.
		* void reset(): empties the buffer and puts every setting back to a new tone_synth's.
		### Adding audio
		* bool note(string note, double length), bool note_ms(string note, int ms): a note by its name, a letter from A to G in either case, then optionally # for sharp or b for flat, then the octave, which can be negative, such as "C4", "f#3" or "Bb2". C4 is middle C, and A4 is 440 hertz. A name that isn't a note, one outside MIDI's range up to G9 once transposed, or one below E0, under the lowest frequency of 20 hertz, is refused.
		* bool note_bend(string note, double bend_amount, double length, double bend_start, double bend_length), and note_bend_ms() with lengths in milliseconds: a note bent by bend_amount semitones, up for a positive amount, starting bend_start into the note and taking bend_length.
		* bool freq(double freq, double length), bool freq_ms(double freq, int ms): a tone of a frequency in hertz, from 20 to 20000.
		* bool freq_bend(double freq, double bend_amount, double length, double bend_start, double bend_length), and freq_bend_ms(): a tone bent by bend_amount hertz.
		* bool rest(double length), bool rest_ms(int ms): moves the position on, writing nothing, so that what is written next starts later; a rest past the end makes the audio that much longer, silent.
		### Reading
		* double length, int length_ms: the length written so far.
		* double position, int position_ms: the current position.
		### Writing out
		* sound@ write_wave_sound(): a sound of the default engine holding the audio, or null if there is nothing to write or the sound system isn't available, as when its context can't be started or another thread is starting it while this runs on an audio thread; it throws, as `sound()` does, if the default engine can't be built.
		* int16[]@ write_samples(): the samples, interleaved left and right, or null.
		* bool write_wave_file(const string&in filename): writes a 16-bit WAV file, replacing a file of that name, and returns whether it was written.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	tone_synth synth;
	synth.tempo = 150;
	// A chord: notes written one after another start at the same position, and mix.
	synth.note("C4", 2);
	synth.note("E4", 2);
	synth.note("G4", 2);
	synth.rest(2); // Past the chord.
	// Then a rising arpeggio, each note followed by a rest as long as it.
	string[] notes = {"C4", "E4", "G4", "C5"};
	for (uint i = 0; i < notes.length(); i++) {
		synth.note(notes[i], 0.5);
		synth.rest(0.5);
	}
	sound@ s = synth.write_wave_sound();
	if (@s == null) return;
	s.play_wait();
}
